      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation, and write down the general solution.",
      "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6(u{x}^{3}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-6u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\nx{w}^{′}+6w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{6}}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{5{x}^{5}}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nSetting ${c}_{1}=-5$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, because $W({y}_{1},{y}_{2})=-5\\ne 0,$ we know that ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent (you should verify this calculation yourself).\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A{x}^{3}+\\frac{B}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$",
